(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("vault",{"vault/what-vault-is-and-the-secrets-problem":{"html":"\u003cp>Secrets sprawl is one of the commonest security weaknesses. The goal of a secrets manager is simple: no long-lived secret in a place a person or a repository can copy, and a record of every access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The problems with static secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Core Vault ideas: engines, auth, policies and leases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What Vault can and cannot protect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vault and its alternatives\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problem\">The problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A static secret has four weaknesses: it is copied (files, chat, wikis), it is shared (many people and services use one password), it is long-lived (rarely rotated), and it is untraceable (nobody knows who used it). Vault attacks each: one store, per-identity access, short leases, and an audit trail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"core-concepts\">Core concepts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Concept\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Secrets engine\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A plugin that stores or generates secrets (KV, database, PKI, transit)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Auth method\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How a caller proves who it is (token, OIDC, Kubernetes, AppRole)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Policy\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rules that say which paths a caller may read or write\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Token\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The credential Vault gives after login, with a time to live\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Lease\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A time limit on a secret; Vault revokes it when it expires\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Audit device\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A log of every request and response (with secrets hashed)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;auth;auth&gt;pol;pol&gt;eng;eng&gt;app;pol&gt;aud\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A request to Vault\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"12.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"auth\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Auth method\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"eng\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secrets engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Auth method; 2, Auth method to Policy check; 3, Policy check to Secrets engine; 4, Secrets engine to Application; 5, Policy check to Audit log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Needs a secret to do its job.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Keeping the secret in a file defeats the point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"auth\">\u003cdt>Auth method \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Verifies the caller's identity and issues a token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A weak login becomes the new weak point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allows or denies the requested path.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wide policies give far too much access.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"eng\">\u003cdt>Secrets engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returns or generates the secret, with a lease.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An engine misconfigured with a root account is risky.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records the request, the caller and the result.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it cannot write, Vault stops answering.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-it-protects\">What it protects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault limits who can read a secret and for how long. It cannot stop an application that has a legitimate secret from misusing it or logging it. Combine it with least privilege, network controls, and short lifetimes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"alternatives\">Alternatives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, Google Secret Manager) are simpler when you live in one cloud. Vault shines across clouds and on premises, and with dynamic secrets and PKI. OpenBao is an open source fork of Vault. Kubernetes Secrets alone are only base64 encoded unless you add encryption at rest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-password-in-six-repositories\">Incident: The password in six repositories\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A departed contractor still knew a live password.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git grep -l &quot;DB_PASSWORD=&quot; -- '*.env' '*.yaml'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git grep -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DB_PASSWORD=&quot;\u003c/span> -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;*.env&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;*.yaml&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing/.env\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">reports/config.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ops/docker-compose.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">... 6 matches in 4 repositories\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The same database password had been copied into config files over three years. Rotating it meant finding every copy. The team moved to Vault dynamic credentials, so each service received its own short-lived login.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating Vault as a nicer place to keep the same shared password\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving every application the same broad policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the audit device\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing the Vault root token in a file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-problem","text":"The problem"},{"id":"core-concepts","text":"Core concepts"},{"id":"what-it-protects","text":"What it protects"},{"id":"alternatives","text":"Alternatives"},{"id":"incident-the-password-in-six-repositories","text":"Incident: The password in six repositories"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Vault documentation","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs"},{"t":"What is Vault","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/what-is-vault"},{"t":"Vault tutorials","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials"}],"text":"Secrets sprawl is one of the commonest security weaknesses. The goal of a secrets manager is simple: no long-lived secret in a place a person or a repository can copy, and a record of every access. What you will learn The problems with static secrets Core Vault ideas: engines, auth, policies and leases What Vault can and cannot protect Vault and its alternatives The problem A static secret has four weaknesses: it is copied (files, chat, wikis), it is shared (many people and services use one password), it is long-lived (rarely rotated), and it is untraceable (nobody knows who used it). Vault attacks each: one store, per-identity access, short leases, and an audit trail. Core concepts Concept Meaning Secrets engine A plugin that stores or generates secrets (KV, database, PKI, transit) Auth method How a caller proves who it is (token, OIDC, Kubernetes, AppRole) Policy Rules that say which paths a caller may read or write Token The credential Vault gives after login, with a time to live Lease A time limit on a secret; Vault revokes it when it expires Audit device A log of every request and response (with secrets hashed) A request to Vault Flow in order: 1, Application to Auth method; 2, Auth method to Policy check; 3, Policy check to Secrets engine; 4, Secrets engine to Application; 5, Policy check to Audit log. Client Security Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Client What it does: Needs a secret to do its job. If it fails or is weak: Keeping the secret in a file defeats the point. Auth method Security What it does: Verifies the caller's identity and issues a token. If it fails or is weak: A weak login becomes the new weak point. Policy check Security What it does: Allows or denies the requested path. If it fails or is weak: Wide policies give far too much access. Secrets engine Data store What it does: Returns or generates the secret, with a lease. If it fails or is weak: An engine misconfigured with a root account is risky. Audit log Observability What it does: Records the request, the caller and the result. If it fails or is weak: If it cannot write, Vault stops answering. What it protects Vault limits who can read a secret and for how long. It cannot stop an application that has a legitimate secret from misusing it or logging it. Combine it with least privilege, network controls, and short lifetimes. Alternatives Cloud secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, Google Secret Manager) are simpler when you live in one cloud. Vault shines across clouds and on premises, and with dynamic secrets and PKI. OpenBao is an open source fork of Vault. Kubernetes Secrets alone are only base64 encoded unless you add encryption at rest. Incident: The password in six repositories Incident A departed contractor still knew a live password. $ git grep -l \"DB_PASSWORD=\" -- &#x27;*.env&#x27; &#x27;*.yaml&#x27; billing/.env reports/config.yaml ops/docker-compose.yaml ... 6 matches in 4 repositories The same database password had been copied into config files over three years. Rotating it meant finding every copy. The team moved to Vault dynamic credentials, so each service received its own short-lived login. Common mistakes Treating Vault as a nicer place to keep the same shared password Giving every application the same broad policy Skipping the audit device Storing the Vault root token in a file"},"vault/starting-vault-seal-unseal-and-storage":{"html":"\u003cp>A running Vault has two states that matter: sealed and unsealed. Understanding them explains most startup problems and most security design.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The encryption barrier and the seal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shamir shares and auto-unseal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a storage backend\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Initialising and protecting the root token\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-barrier-and-the-seal\">The barrier and the seal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault writes data to storage through an \u003cstrong>encryption barrier\u003c/strong>. Everything past the barrier is encrypted with a data encryption key. That key is encrypted by the root key, and the root key is protected by the \u003cstrong>seal\u003c/strong>. When Vault is sealed it knows where its data is but cannot decrypt it. Unsealing gives it the root key in memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unsealing\">Unsealing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default, initialisation splits the root key into key shares with Shamir&#39;s algorithm. A threshold of shares, for example 3 of 5, is needed to unseal. Give shares to different people.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3\nvault operator unseal\nvault status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3\nvault operator unseal\nvault status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Auto-unseal\u003c/strong> delegates this to a trusted external system: a cloud KMS, an HSM, or another Vault&#39;s transit engine. Vault unseals itself at start without a person. With auto-unseal you receive recovery keys, which are used for sensitive operations rather than unsealing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"disk&gt;root;shares&gt;root;kms&gt;root;root&gt;vault\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How Vault unseals\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Encrypted storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"root\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Root key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"shares\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shamir key shares\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kms\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud KMS or HSM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vault\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Unsealed Vault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Encrypted storage to Root key; 2, Shamir key shares to Root key; 3, Cloud KMS or HSM to Root key; 4, Root key to Unsealed Vault.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disk\">\u003cdt>Encrypted storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds all data encrypted by the barrier.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stolen storage alone is unreadable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"root\">\u003cdt>Root key \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Protects the data key; kept only in memory when unsealed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never written to disk in clear.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"shares\">\u003cdt>Shamir key shares \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A threshold of shares rebuilds the root key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost shares below threshold mean a permanent seal.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kms\">\u003cdt>Cloud KMS or HSM \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Auto-unseal: the external key wraps the root key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the KMS is unreachable after a restart Vault stays sealed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vault\">\u003cdt>Unsealed Vault \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves requests with the keys in memory.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A memory dump of an unsealed node exposes keys.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-backends\">Storage backends\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Integrated storage (Raft)\u003c/strong> keeps data on each node&#39;s disk and replicates among nodes. It needs no extra system and is the recommended choice. Older setups used Consul. Cloud backends exist but give fewer features. Use fast local disks, and back up with Raft snapshots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"initial-setup\">Initial setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>vault operator init\u003c/code> prints the first root token once. Use it only to configure authentication and policies, then revoke it. Day-to-day work should use named identities. Dev mode (\u003ccode>vault server -dev\u003c/code>) is in memory, auto-unsealed, with a known token: great for learning, never for real secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-sealed-all-night\">Incident: Sealed all night\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node restart left Vault unreachable until morning.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault status\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Key                Value\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Seal Type          shamir\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Initialized        true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sealed             true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unseal Progress    1/3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After a kernel patch rebooted the nodes, Vault started sealed. Only one of the five key holders was reachable. The team configured auto-unseal with a cloud KMS key and tested a restart during working hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running dev mode anywhere real\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving all key shares to one person\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the initial root token active\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing a restart\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-barrier-and-the-seal","text":"The barrier and the seal"},{"id":"unsealing","text":"Unsealing"},{"id":"storage-backends","text":"Storage backends"},{"id":"initial-setup","text":"Initial setup"},{"id":"incident-sealed-all-night","text":"Incident: Sealed all night"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Seal and unseal","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/seal"},{"t":"Integrated storage","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/configuration/storage/raft"},{"t":"Vault server configuration","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/configuration"}],"text":"A running Vault has two states that matter: sealed and unsealed. Understanding them explains most startup problems and most security design. What you will learn The encryption barrier and the seal Shamir shares and auto-unseal Choosing a storage backend Initialising and protecting the root token The barrier and the seal Vault writes data to storage through an encryption barrier . Everything past the barrier is encrypted with a data encryption key. That key is encrypted by the root key, and the root key is protected by the seal . When Vault is sealed it knows where its data is but cannot decrypt it. Unsealing gives it the root key in memory. Unsealing By default, initialisation splits the root key into key shares with Shamir&#39;s algorithm. A threshold of shares, for example 3 of 5, is needed to unseal. Give shares to different people. vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3 vault operator unseal vault status Auto-unseal delegates this to a trusted external system: a cloud KMS, an HSM, or another Vault&#39;s transit engine. Vault unseals itself at start without a person. With auto-unseal you receive recovery keys, which are used for sensitive operations rather than unsealing. How Vault unseals Flow in order: 1, Encrypted storage to Root key; 2, Shamir key shares to Root key; 3, Cloud KMS or HSM to Root key; 4, Root key to Unsealed Vault. Data store Security Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Encrypted storage Data store What it does: Holds all data encrypted by the barrier. If it fails or is weak: Stolen storage alone is unreadable. Root key Security What it does: Protects the data key; kept only in memory when unsealed. If it fails or is weak: Never written to disk in clear. Shamir key shares Security What it does: A threshold of shares rebuilds the root key. If it fails or is weak: Lost shares below threshold mean a permanent seal. Cloud KMS or HSM Outside party What it does: Auto-unseal: the external key wraps the root key. If it fails or is weak: If the KMS is unreachable after a restart Vault stays sealed. Unsealed Vault Service What it does: Serves requests with the keys in memory. If it fails or is weak: A memory dump of an unsealed node exposes keys. Storage backends Integrated storage (Raft) keeps data on each node&#39;s disk and replicates among nodes. It needs no extra system and is the recommended choice. Older setups used Consul. Cloud backends exist but give fewer features. Use fast local disks, and back up with Raft snapshots. Initial setup vault operator init prints the first root token once. Use it only to configure authentication and policies, then revoke it. Day-to-day work should use named identities. Dev mode ( vault server -dev ) is in memory, auto-unsealed, with a known token: great for learning, never for real secrets. Incident: Sealed all night Incident A node restart left Vault unreachable until morning. $ vault status Key Value Seal Type shamir Initialized true Sealed true Unseal Progress 1/3 After a kernel patch rebooted the nodes, Vault started sealed. Only one of the five key holders was reachable. The team configured auto-unseal with a cloud KMS key and tested a restart during working hours. Common mistakes Running dev mode anywhere real Giving all key shares to one person Leaving the initial root token active Not testing a restart"},"vault/authentication-methods-and-tokens":{"html":"\u003cp>Every Vault request needs a token. How a caller gets one decides how strong the whole system is. The ideal is a login that does not require a secret you must store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Methods for people and for machines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tokens, TTLs and renewal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>AppRole and the secret-zero problem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Identity based methods\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"methods\">Methods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>For\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How it proves identity\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>OIDC / LDAP\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>People\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Single sign-on through the company identity provider\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Kubernetes\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pods\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The pod&#39;s service account token\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>AWS / Azure / GCP\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cloud workloads\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The cloud&#39;s signed identity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>AppRole\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pipelines and other machines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A role ID plus a secret ID\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Token / userpass\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bootstrap and tests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A direct credential\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Enable and use a method:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault auth enable oidc\nvault auth enable kubernetes\nvault auth list\nvault login -method=oidc\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault auth \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> oidc\nvault auth \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> kubernetes\nvault auth list\nvault login -method=oidc\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokens\">Tokens\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A login returns a \u003cstrong>token\u003c/strong> with policies and a \u003cstrong>TTL\u003c/strong>. A service token can be renewed until its max TTL. A periodic token renews forever as long as it renews in time. A batch token is lightweight with no storage. Child tokens are revoked when their parent is, unless created as orphans. Set short TTLs so a leaked token expires quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"who&gt;method;method&gt;ident;method&gt;tok;tok&gt;use\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From login to secret\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"who\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Person or workload\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"method\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Auth method\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tok\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Token with policies and TTL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ident\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity provider\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"use\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request to a secrets engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Person or workload to Auth method; 2, Auth method to Identity provider; 3, Auth method to Token with policies and TTL; 4, Token with policies and TTL to Request to a secrets engine.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"who\">\u003cdt>Person or workload \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Presents proof of identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A stolen proof lets someone log in.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"method\">\u003cdt>Auth method \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks the proof against the identity source.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A loose binding accepts the wrong caller.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tok\">\u003cdt>Token with policies and TTL \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Short lived and renewable up to a maximum.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Long TTLs turn a leak into a long breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ident\">\u003cdt>Identity provider \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> OIDC, Kubernetes or cloud IAM.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Its own security sets the ceiling.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"use\">\u003cdt>Request to a secrets engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The token is sent with each call.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tokens in logs are credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"approle-and-secret-zero\">AppRole and secret zero\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AppRole splits login into a role ID (not secret, lives with the app) and a secret ID (delivered by the pipeline, single use, short lived). That reduces risk but still needs a trusted way to deliver the secret ID. Platform identity methods avoid this &quot;secret zero&quot; problem entirely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"binding\">Binding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Bind roles tightly: a Kubernetes role should name the exact service account and namespace, and a cloud role should name the exact IAM role, not a wildcard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-any-pod-could-log-in\">Incident: Any pod could log in\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A test pod read production secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault read auth/kubernetes/role/app\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> auth/kubernetes/role/app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Key                                 Value\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bound_service_account_names         [*]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bound_service_account_namespaces    [*]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">policies                            [prod-db]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role accepted every service account in every namespace, so a pod in a test namespace could obtain the production policy. The role was bound to one service account in the production namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wildcard bindings on Kubernetes or cloud roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Very long token TTLs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared AppRole secret IDs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the root token for applications\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"methods","text":"Methods"},{"id":"tokens","text":"Tokens"},{"id":"approle-and-secret-zero","text":"AppRole and secret zero"},{"id":"binding","text":"Binding"},{"id":"incident-any-pod-could-log-in","text":"Incident: Any pod could log in"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Auth methods","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/auth"},{"t":"Tokens","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/tokens"},{"t":"Kubernetes auth method","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/auth/kubernetes"}],"text":"Every Vault request needs a token. How a caller gets one decides how strong the whole system is. The ideal is a login that does not require a secret you must store. What you will learn Methods for people and for machines Tokens, TTLs and renewal AppRole and the secret-zero problem Identity based methods Methods Method For How it proves identity OIDC / LDAP People Single sign-on through the company identity provider Kubernetes Pods The pod&#39;s service account token AWS / Azure / GCP Cloud workloads The cloud&#39;s signed identity AppRole Pipelines and other machines A role ID plus a secret ID Token / userpass Bootstrap and tests A direct credential Enable and use a method: vault auth enable oidc vault auth enable kubernetes vault auth list vault login -method=oidc Tokens A login returns a token with policies and a TTL . A service token can be renewed until its max TTL. A periodic token renews forever as long as it renews in time. A batch token is lightweight with no storage. Child tokens are revoked when their parent is, unless created as orphans. Set short TTLs so a leaked token expires quickly. From login to secret Flow in order: 1, Person or workload to Auth method; 2, Auth method to Identity provider; 3, Auth method to Token with policies and TTL; 4, Token with policies and TTL to Request to a secrets engine. Client Security Outside party Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Person or workload Client What it does: Presents proof of identity. If it fails or is weak: A stolen proof lets someone log in. Auth method Security What it does: Checks the proof against the identity source. If it fails or is weak: A loose binding accepts the wrong caller. Token with policies and TTL Security What it does: Short lived and renewable up to a maximum. If it fails or is weak: Long TTLs turn a leak into a long breach. Identity provider Outside party What it does: OIDC, Kubernetes or cloud IAM. If it fails or is weak: Its own security sets the ceiling. Request to a secrets engine Service What it does: The token is sent with each call. If it fails or is weak: Tokens in logs are credentials. AppRole and secret zero AppRole splits login into a role ID (not secret, lives with the app) and a secret ID (delivered by the pipeline, single use, short lived). That reduces risk but still needs a trusted way to deliver the secret ID. Platform identity methods avoid this \"secret zero\" problem entirely. Binding Bind roles tightly: a Kubernetes role should name the exact service account and namespace, and a cloud role should name the exact IAM role, not a wildcard. Incident: Any pod could log in Incident A test pod read production secrets. $ vault read auth/kubernetes/role/app Key Value bound_service_account_names [*] bound_service_account_namespaces [*] policies [prod-db] The role accepted every service account in every namespace, so a pod in a test namespace could obtain the production policy. The role was bound to one service account in the production namespace. Common mistakes Wildcard bindings on Kubernetes or cloud roles Very long token TTLs Shared AppRole secret IDs Using the root token for applications"},"vault/policies-and-least-privilege-access":{"html":"\u003cp>Authentication says who you are. Policies say what you may do. Most real Vault incidents come from policies that are too broad, not from broken cryptography.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Path based policies and capabilities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>KV version 2 data and metadata paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Templated policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing and reviewing policies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-policy\">A policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"path &quot;secret/data/billing/*&quot; {\n  capabilities = [&quot;read&quot;]\n}\npath &quot;secret/metadata/billing/*&quot; {\n  capabilities = [&quot;list&quot;]\n}\npath &quot;database/creds/billing-ro&quot; {\n  capabilities = [&quot;read&quot;]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">path\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;secret/data/billing/*&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  capabilities\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">path\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;secret/metadata/billing/*&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  capabilities\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">path\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database/creds/billing-ro&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  capabilities\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Capabilities: \u003ccode>create\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>read\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>update\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>delete\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> (for protected paths) and \u003ccode>deny\u003c/code> (overrides everything). A path ending in \u003ccode>*\u003c/code> matches any suffix. If no policy mentions a path, access is denied.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kv-v2-paths\">KV v2 paths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With KV version 2, the API path differs from the CLI path. \u003ccode>vault kv get secret/billing/db\u003c/code> reads \u003ccode>secret/data/billing/db\u003c/code> underneath. Listing uses \u003ccode>secret/metadata/...\u003c/code>. A policy that names the CLI path without \u003ccode>data/\u003c/code> silently gives no access, a common first-day mistake.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"req&gt;p1;req&gt;p2;p1&gt;dec;p2&gt;dec;dec&gt;aud\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Policy decision for one request\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request path + token\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"p1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy billing-read\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"p2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dec\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allow or deny\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Request path + token to Policy billing-read; 2, Request path + token to Policy default; 3, Policy billing-read to Allow or deny; 4, Policy default to Allow or deny; 5, Allow or deny to Audit log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Request path + token \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A read of secret/data/billing/db.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The token's policies are all that count.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"p1\">\u003cdt>Policy billing-read \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allows read under secret/data/billing/*.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too wide a glob adds access by accident.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"p2\">\u003cdt>Policy default \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Basic self-service paths.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The default policy applies to every token.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dec\">\u003cdt>Allow or deny \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Union of capabilities, with deny winning.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No match means denied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records the decision and the caller.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Review denied requests for misconfigurations.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templated-policies\">Templated policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"hcl\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"path &quot;secret/data/{{identity.entity.aliases.auth_kubernetes_abc.metadata.service_account_namespace}}/*&quot; {\n  capabilities = [&quot;read&quot;]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">path\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;secret/data/{{identity.entity.aliases.auth_kubernetes_abc.metadata.service_account_namespace}}/*&quot;\u003c/span> {\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">  capabilities\u003c/span> = [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read&quot;\u003c/span>]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Templating pulls values from the caller&#39;s identity, so one policy gives each namespace its own area without a separate policy per team.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"testing\">Testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault policy write billing-read billing-read.hcl\nvault token create -policy=billing-read -ttl=15m\nVAULT_TOKEN=&lt;new token&gt; vault kv get secret/billing/db\nvault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault policy write billing-read billing-read.hcl\nvault token create -policy=billing-read -ttl=15m\nVAULT_TOKEN=&lt;new token&gt; vault kv get secret/billing/db\nvault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Create a short-lived token with only the new policy and try allowed and forbidden paths. Review policies in version control like code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-read-on-everything\">Incident: Read on everything\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A breached service read other teams&#39; secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault policy read app\nvault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault policy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">path &quot;secret/*&quot; {\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  capabilities = [&quot;read&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">list, read\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shared policy was written in a hurry and attached to every service. When one service was compromised, the attacker could read all paths. The team created one policy per application, limited to its own prefix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One broad policy for every application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting the data prefix in KV version 2\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting sudo or write where read is enough\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not putting policies in version control\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-policy","text":"A policy"},{"id":"kv-v2-paths","text":"KV v2 paths"},{"id":"templated-policies","text":"Templated policies"},{"id":"testing","text":"Testing"},{"id":"incident-read-on-everything","text":"Incident: Read on everything"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Policies","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/policies"},{"t":"Policy tutorial","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/policies"},{"t":"KV version 2","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/kv/kv-v2"}],"text":"Authentication says who you are. Policies say what you may do. Most real Vault incidents come from policies that are too broad, not from broken cryptography. What you will learn Path based policies and capabilities KV version 2 data and metadata paths Templated policies Testing and reviewing policies A policy path \"secret/data/billing/*\" { capabilities = [ \"read\" ] } path \"secret/metadata/billing/*\" { capabilities = [ \"list\" ] } path \"database/creds/billing-ro\" { capabilities = [ \"read\" ] } Capabilities: create , read , update , delete , list , sudo (for protected paths) and deny (overrides everything). A path ending in * matches any suffix. If no policy mentions a path, access is denied. KV v2 paths With KV version 2, the API path differs from the CLI path. vault kv get secret/billing/db reads secret/data/billing/db underneath. Listing uses secret/metadata/... . A policy that names the CLI path without data/ silently gives no access, a common first-day mistake. Policy decision for one request Flow in order: 1, Request path + token to Policy billing-read; 2, Request path + token to Policy default; 3, Policy billing-read to Allow or deny; 4, Policy default to Allow or deny; 5, Allow or deny to Audit log. Client Security Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Request path + token Client What it does: A read of secret/data/billing/db. If it fails or is weak: The token's policies are all that count. Policy billing-read Security What it does: Allows read under secret/data/billing/*. If it fails or is weak: Too wide a glob adds access by accident. Policy default Security What it does: Basic self-service paths. If it fails or is weak: The default policy applies to every token. Allow or deny Step What it does: Union of capabilities, with deny winning. If it fails or is weak: No match means denied. Audit log Observability What it does: Records the decision and the caller. If it fails or is weak: Review denied requests for misconfigurations. Templated policies path \"secret/data/{{identity.entity.aliases.auth_kubernetes_abc.metadata.service_account_namespace}}/*\" { capabilities = [ \"read\" ] } Templating pulls values from the caller&#39;s identity, so one policy gives each namespace its own area without a separate policy per team. Testing vault policy write billing-read billing-read.hcl vault token create -policy=billing-read -ttl=15m VAULT_TOKEN=\u003cnew token> vault kv get secret/billing/db vault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db Create a short-lived token with only the new policy and try allowed and forbidden paths. Review policies in version control like code. Incident: Read on everything Incident A breached service read other teams&#39; secrets. $ vault policy read app path \"secret/*\" { capabilities = [\"read\", \"list\"] } $ vault token capabilities secret/data/payments/db list, read The shared policy was written in a hurry and attached to every service. When one service was compromised, the attacker could read all paths. The team created one policy per application, limited to its own prefix. Common mistakes One broad policy for every application Forgetting the data prefix in KV version 2 Granting sudo or write where read is enough Not putting policies in version control"},"vault/kv-secrets-engine-and-secret-versioning":{"html":"\u003cp>The KV engine is the simplest and most used. It is a good home for secrets that exist outside Vault, such as third party API keys, and it adds versioning and audit that a file never had.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enabling KV and reading and writing secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versions, soft delete and destroy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patch and check-and-set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Organising paths and avoiding sprawl\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"using-kv\">Using KV\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2\nvault kv put secret/billing/stripe api_key=sk_live_example\nvault kv get secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv get -field=api_key secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv patch secret/billing/stripe webhook=whsec_example\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault secrets \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> -path=secret kv-v2\nvault kv put secret/billing/stripe api_key=sk_live_example\nvault kv get secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv get -field=api_key secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv patch secret/billing/stripe webhook=whsec_example\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code> replaces the whole secret with a new version. \u003ccode>patch\u003c/code> updates only the keys you give and keeps the rest. Always prefer \u003ccode>patch\u003c/code> for partial changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions\">Versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault kv get -version=2 secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv metadata get secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv delete secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv undelete -versions=3 secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv destroy -versions=1 secret/billing/stripe\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault kv get -version=2 secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv metadata get secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv delete secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv undelete -versions=3 secret/billing/stripe\nvault kv destroy -versions=1 secret/billing/stripe\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>delete\u003c/code> marks a version deleted but keeps it for recovery. \u003ccode>destroy\u003c/code> removes it for good. Set \u003ccode>max_versions\u003c/code> so history does not grow forever.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"w1&gt;w2;w2&gt;soft;soft&gt;dest;cas&gt;w2\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A secret and its versions\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"w1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Write: version 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"w2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Write: version 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"soft\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Soft delete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dest\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Destroy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cas\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Check and set\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Write: version 1 to Write: version 2; 2, Write: version 2 to Soft delete; 3, Soft delete to Destroy; 4, Check and set to Write: version 2.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"w1\">\u003cdt>Write: version 1 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> First value stored.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old values may be secret too.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"w2\">\u003cdt>Write: version 2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Changed API key.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Services still reading version 1 keep the old key.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"soft\">\u003cdt>Soft delete \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Version marked deleted but recoverable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Looks gone but still exists.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dest\">\u003cdt>Destroy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Version data is permanently removed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cannot be undone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cas\">\u003cdt>Check and set \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A write must name the current version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it concurrent writers overwrite each other.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-and-set\">Check-and-set\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>-cas=&lt;current version&gt;\u003c/code>, a write fails if someone else wrote in the meantime. You can require it for a whole mount with \u003ccode>cas_required\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"paths\">Paths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a consistent layout such as \u003ccode>secret/&lt;team&gt;/&lt;app&gt;/&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code>. Match it to policies, so a team&#39;s access is one prefix. Do not store large files or high-rate data in KV. It is for small secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-not-to-use-it\">When not to use it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the target supports dynamic credentials (databases, cloud IAM), use those instead. A static password in KV is still a long-lived secret, only better protected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-overwritten-key\">Incident: The overwritten key\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deploy failed because a secret key had disappeared.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault kv get -version=6 secret/app\nvault kv get -version=7 secret/app\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault kv get -version=6 secret/app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api_key    sk_live_example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">webhook    whsec_example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault kv get -version=7 secret/app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api_key    sk_live_example2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Version 7 had been written with put and only one key, so the webhook key vanished. The team recovered it from version 6, then moved everyone to patch and enabled cas_required on the mount.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using put when patch was meant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never setting max_versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing large files in KV\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping static database passwords in KV when dynamic ones are possible\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"using-kv","text":"Using KV"},{"id":"versions","text":"Versions"},{"id":"check-and-set","text":"Check-and-set"},{"id":"paths","text":"Paths"},{"id":"when-not-to-use-it","text":"When not to use it"},{"id":"incident-the-overwritten-key","text":"Incident: The overwritten key"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KV secrets engine","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/kv"},{"t":"KV version 2","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/kv/kv-v2"},{"t":"Static secrets tutorial","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/secrets-management/static-secrets"}],"text":"The KV engine is the simplest and most used. It is a good home for secrets that exist outside Vault, such as third party API keys, and it adds versioning and audit that a file never had. What you will learn Enabling KV and reading and writing secrets Versions, soft delete and destroy Patch and check-and-set Organising paths and avoiding sprawl Using KV vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2 vault kv put secret/billing/stripe api_key=sk_live_example vault kv get secret/billing/stripe vault kv get -field=api_key secret/billing/stripe vault kv patch secret/billing/stripe webhook=whsec_example put replaces the whole secret with a new version. patch updates only the keys you give and keeps the rest. Always prefer patch for partial changes. Versions vault kv get -version=2 secret/billing/stripe vault kv metadata get secret/billing/stripe vault kv delete secret/billing/stripe vault kv undelete -versions=3 secret/billing/stripe vault kv destroy -versions=1 secret/billing/stripe delete marks a version deleted but keeps it for recovery. destroy removes it for good. Set max_versions so history does not grow forever. A secret and its versions Flow in order: 1, Write: version 1 to Write: version 2; 2, Write: version 2 to Soft delete; 3, Soft delete to Destroy; 4, Check and set to Write: version 2. Data store Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Write: version 1 Data store What it does: First value stored. If it fails or is weak: Old values may be secret too. Write: version 2 Data store What it does: Changed API key. If it fails or is weak: Services still reading version 1 keep the old key. Soft delete Step What it does: Version marked deleted but recoverable. If it fails or is weak: Looks gone but still exists. Destroy Step What it does: Version data is permanently removed. If it fails or is weak: Cannot be undone. Check and set Security What it does: A write must name the current version. If it fails or is weak: Without it concurrent writers overwrite each other. Check-and-set With -cas=\u003ccurrent version> , a write fails if someone else wrote in the meantime. You can require it for a whole mount with cas_required . Paths Use a consistent layout such as secret/\u003cteam>/\u003capp>/\u003cname> . Match it to policies, so a team&#39;s access is one prefix. Do not store large files or high-rate data in KV. It is for small secrets. When not to use it If the target supports dynamic credentials (databases, cloud IAM), use those instead. A static password in KV is still a long-lived secret, only better protected. Incident: The overwritten key Incident A deploy failed because a secret key had disappeared. $ vault kv get -version=6 secret/app api_key sk_live_example webhook whsec_example $ vault kv get -version=7 secret/app api_key sk_live_example2 Version 7 had been written with put and only one key, so the webhook key vanished. The team recovered it from version 6, then moved everyone to patch and enabled cas_required on the mount. Common mistakes Using put when patch was meant Never setting max_versions Storing large files in KV Keeping static database passwords in KV when dynamic ones are possible"},"vault/dynamic-secrets-for-databases-and-clouds":{"html":"\u003cp>Dynamic secrets are Vault&#39;s best idea. Nothing to store, nothing to rotate by hand, nothing shared. Each service gets its own login that expires.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How dynamic secrets work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configuring the database engine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leases, renewal and revocation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloud credentials and rotating the root user\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-idea\">The idea\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The application asks Vault for credentials. Vault creates a user in the database with a random password and a limited set of grants, returns it with a lease, and removes it when the lease expires or is revoked. Each request gets a different user, so the audit log can name the caller.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configuring-a-database\">Configuring a database\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault secrets enable database\nvault write database/config/orders \\\n  plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \\\n  connection_url=&quot;postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@db.internal:5432/orders&quot; \\\n  allowed_roles=&quot;orders-ro&quot; \\\n  username=&quot;vault_admin&quot; password=&quot;change-me&quot;\nvault write database/roles/orders-ro \\\n  db_name=orders \\\n  creation_statements=&quot;CREATE ROLE \\&quot;{{name}}\\&quot; WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \\&quot;{{name}}\\&quot;;&quot; \\\n  default_ttl=1h max_ttl=24h\nvault read database/creds/orders-ro\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault secrets \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> database\nvault write database/config/orders \\\n  plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \\\n  connection_url=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@db.internal:5432/orders&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  allowed_roles=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders-ro&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  username=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vault_admin&quot;\u003c/span> password=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;change-me&quot;\u003c/span>\nvault write database/roles/orders-ro \\\n  db_name=orders \\\n  creation_statements=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CREATE ROLE \\&quot;{{name}}\\&quot; WITH LOGIN PASSWORD &#x27;{{password}}&#x27; VALID UNTIL &#x27;{{expiration}}&#x27;; GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \\&quot;{{name}}\\&quot;;&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  default_ttl=1h max_ttl=24h\nvault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> database/creds/orders-ro\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;vault;vault&gt;db;vault&gt;lease;lease&gt;rev;rev&gt;db\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A dynamic database credential\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vault\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vault database engine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lease\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lease with TTL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revocation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Vault database engine; 2, Vault database engine to Database; 3, Vault database engine to Lease with TTL; 4, Lease with TTL to Revocation; 5, Revocation to Database.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests credentials at startup or before expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Caching them forever defeats the lease.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vault\">\u003cdt>Vault database engine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the creation SQL with its admin account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The admin account is a high value target.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the new temporary user.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Users left behind show a failed revoke.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lease\">\u003cdt>Lease with TTL \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tracks the credential until it expires.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Renewing beyond max TTL is refused.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Revocation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Drops the user at expiry or on demand.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Revoke by prefix after a breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leases\">Leases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each credential has a lease ID. The application renews it before it expires, up to the max TTL, then asks for new ones. \u003ccode>vault lease revoke &lt;id&gt;\u003c/code> deletes one user. \u003ccode>vault lease revoke -prefix database/creds/orders-ro\u003c/code> deletes all of them, a strong response to a suspected leak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotating-the-root-user\">Rotating the root user\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault needs a privileged database account. Run \u003ccode>vault write -f database/rotate-root/orders\u003c/code> and Vault changes that account&#39;s password to one only it knows, so even the person who set it up no longer has it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloud\">Cloud\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The AWS engine can create IAM users or, better, issue temporary STS credentials for a role. Azure and Google engines work similarly. Set short TTLs and narrow roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-credential-that-died-on-schedule\">Incident: The credential that died on schedule\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A leaked connection string was already useless.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault read database/creds/orders-ro\npsql -U v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442 -h db.internal orders\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> database/creds/orders-ro\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Key                Value\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lease_duration     1h\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">username           v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">password           A1b-example-only\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -U v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442 -h db.internal orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  role &quot;v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442&quot; does not exist\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A developer pasted a connection string into a public issue tracker. By the time a scanner found it, the TTL had passed and Vault had dropped the database user. The team only needed to confirm in the audit log that nothing used it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving the Vault admin account more rights than it needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long TTLs on dynamic credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching credentials past their lease\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not rotating the root credential\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-idea","text":"The idea"},{"id":"configuring-a-database","text":"Configuring a database"},{"id":"leases","text":"Leases"},{"id":"rotating-the-root-user","text":"Rotating the root user"},{"id":"cloud","text":"Cloud"},{"id":"incident-the-credential-that-died-on-schedule","text":"Incident: The credential that died on schedule"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Database secrets engine","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/databases"},{"t":"AWS secrets engine","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/aws"},{"t":"Dynamic secrets tutorial","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/db-credentials"}],"text":"Dynamic secrets are Vault&#39;s best idea. Nothing to store, nothing to rotate by hand, nothing shared. Each service gets its own login that expires. What you will learn How dynamic secrets work Configuring the database engine Leases, renewal and revocation Cloud credentials and rotating the root user The idea The application asks Vault for credentials. Vault creates a user in the database with a random password and a limited set of grants, returns it with a lease, and removes it when the lease expires or is revoked. Each request gets a different user, so the audit log can name the caller. Configuring a database vault secrets enable database vault write database/config/orders \\ plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \\ connection_url= \"postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@db.internal:5432/orders\" \\ allowed_roles= \"orders-ro\" \\ username= \"vault_admin\" password= \"change-me\" vault write database/roles/orders-ro \\ db_name=orders \\ creation_statements= \"CREATE ROLE \\\"{{name}}\\\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD &#x27;{{password}}&#x27; VALID UNTIL &#x27;{{expiration}}&#x27;; GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \\\"{{name}}\\\";\" \\ default_ttl=1h max_ttl=24h vault read database/creds/orders-ro A dynamic database credential Flow in order: 1, Application to Vault database engine; 2, Vault database engine to Database; 3, Vault database engine to Lease with TTL; 4, Lease with TTL to Revocation; 5, Revocation to Database. Client Security Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Client What it does: Requests credentials at startup or before expiry. If it fails or is weak: Caching them forever defeats the lease. Vault database engine Security What it does: Runs the creation SQL with its admin account. If it fails or is weak: The admin account is a high value target. Database Data store What it does: Holds the new temporary user. If it fails or is weak: Users left behind show a failed revoke. Lease with TTL Step What it does: Tracks the credential until it expires. If it fails or is weak: Renewing beyond max TTL is refused. Revocation Step What it does: Drops the user at expiry or on demand. If it fails or is weak: Revoke by prefix after a breach. Leases Each credential has a lease ID. The application renews it before it expires, up to the max TTL, then asks for new ones. vault lease revoke \u003cid> deletes one user. vault lease revoke -prefix database/creds/orders-ro deletes all of them, a strong response to a suspected leak. Rotating the root user Vault needs a privileged database account. Run vault write -f database/rotate-root/orders and Vault changes that account&#39;s password to one only it knows, so even the person who set it up no longer has it. Cloud The AWS engine can create IAM users or, better, issue temporary STS credentials for a role. Azure and Google engines work similarly. Set short TTLs and narrow roles. Incident: The credential that died on schedule Incident A leaked connection string was already useless. $ vault read database/creds/orders-ro Key Value lease_duration 1h username v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442 password A1b-example-only $ psql -U v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442 -h db.internal orders FATAL: role \"v-oidc-orders-ro-1m3k9x-1730871442\" does not exist A developer pasted a connection string into a public issue tracker. By the time a scanner found it, the TTL had passed and Vault had dropped the database user. The team only needed to confirm in the audit log that nothing used it. Common mistakes Giving the Vault admin account more rights than it needs Long TTLs on dynamic credentials Caching credentials past their lease Not rotating the root credential"},"vault/pki-short-lived-certificates-with-vault":{"html":"\u003cp>Mutual TLS between services needs a steady supply of certificates. Doing that by hand does not scale. A private CA that issues short-lived certificates through an API does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Root and intermediate CAs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles that limit what can be issued\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Issuing and renewing certificates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Revocation, CRLs and ACME\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hierarchy\">Hierarchy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>root CA\u003c/strong> signs only intermediates and should be rarely used, kept offline or in its own protected mount. An \u003cstrong>intermediate CA\u003c/strong> signs the leaf certificates that services use. If the intermediate is compromised you replace it; the root stays trusted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault secrets enable -path=pki pki\nvault secrets tune -max-lease-ttl=87600h pki\nvault write pki/root/generate/internal common_name=&quot;Example Root&quot; ttl=87600h\nvault secrets enable -path=pki_int pki\nvault write pki_int/intermediate/generate/internal common_name=&quot;Example Intermediate&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault secrets \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> -path=pki pki\nvault secrets tune -max-lease-ttl=87600h pki\nvault write pki/root/generate/internal common_name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Example Root&quot;\u003c/span> ttl=87600h\nvault secrets \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> -path=pki_int pki\nvault write pki_int/intermediate/generate/internal common_name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Example Intermediate&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>You then sign the intermediate&#39;s request with the root and set the signed certificate back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles\">Roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault write pki_int/roles/internal-svc \\\n  allowed_domains=&quot;svc.example.internal&quot; \\\n  allow_subdomains=true \\\n  max_ttl=72h\nvault write pki_int/issue/internal-svc common_name=api.svc.example.internal ttl=24h\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault write pki_int/roles/internal-svc \\\n  allowed_domains=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;svc.example.internal&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  allow_subdomains=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span> \\\n  max_ttl=72h\nvault write pki_int/issue/internal-svc common_name=api.svc.example.internal ttl=24h\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A role says which names a caller may request and the longest lifetime. Policies decide who may use a role.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"root&gt;int;int&gt;role;role&gt;svc;root&gt;trust;svc&gt;trust\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A private CA with Vault\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"root\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Root CA (offline)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"int\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Intermediate CA in Vault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">PKI role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trust\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trust bundle\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Root CA (offline) to Intermediate CA in Vault; 2, Intermediate CA in Vault to PKI role; 3, PKI role to Service; 4, Root CA (offline) to Trust bundle; 5, Service to Trust bundle.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"root\">\u003cdt>Root CA (offline) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs the intermediate, then stays out of daily use.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A leaked root key means rebuilding all trust.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"int\">\u003cdt>Intermediate CA in Vault \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs service certificates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Its key is the one used every day.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>PKI role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limits domains, key types and TTL.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wildcard role can issue for any name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests and renews its certificate.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing renewal still causes expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trust\">\u003cdt>Trust bundle \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The root certificate distributed to clients.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Clients that lack it reject the chain.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"issuing-and-renewing\">Issuing and renewing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Applications ask for a certificate, or a sidecar or agent does it and writes files for the service. Because lifetimes are short, the agent renews at about two thirds of the lifetime. Cert-manager in Kubernetes can use Vault as an issuer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"revocation\">Revocation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With a 24 hour life, waiting for expiry is often enough. For longer lifetimes Vault keeps a CRL and can serve OCSP. Newer Vault versions support ACME, so standard ACME clients can request certificates from your internal CA.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-weekend-expiry\">Incident: The weekend expiry\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An internal API stopped because its certificate had expired.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo | openssl s_client -connect api.internal:443 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\ndate -u\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> | openssl s_client -connect api.internal:443 2&gt;/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">notAfter=Nov  2 00:00:00 2026 GMT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> -u\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sat Nov  7 08:10:00 UTC 2026\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The certificate had a one year lifetime and renewal was a manual calendar task nobody owned. The team moved to Vault issued certificates with a 24 hour TTL, renewed by an agent every 16 hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Issuing leaf certificates directly from the root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roles that allow any domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long lifetimes without a revocation plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not alerting when renewal fails\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hierarchy","text":"Hierarchy"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles"},{"id":"issuing-and-renewing","text":"Issuing and renewing"},{"id":"revocation","text":"Revocation"},{"id":"incident-the-weekend-expiry","text":"Incident: The weekend expiry"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PKI secrets engine","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/pki"},{"t":"PKI tutorial","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/secrets-management/pki-engine"},{"t":"Vault documentation","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs"}],"text":"Mutual TLS between services needs a steady supply of certificates. Doing that by hand does not scale. A private CA that issues short-lived certificates through an API does. What you will learn Root and intermediate CAs Roles that limit what can be issued Issuing and renewing certificates Revocation, CRLs and ACME Hierarchy A root CA signs only intermediates and should be rarely used, kept offline or in its own protected mount. An intermediate CA signs the leaf certificates that services use. If the intermediate is compromised you replace it; the root stays trusted. vault secrets enable -path=pki pki vault secrets tune -max-lease-ttl=87600h pki vault write pki/root/generate/internal common_name= \"Example Root\" ttl=87600h vault secrets enable -path=pki_int pki vault write pki_int/intermediate/generate/internal common_name= \"Example Intermediate\" You then sign the intermediate&#39;s request with the root and set the signed certificate back. Roles vault write pki_int/roles/internal-svc \\ allowed_domains= \"svc.example.internal\" \\ allow_subdomains= true \\ max_ttl=72h vault write pki_int/issue/internal-svc common_name=api.svc.example.internal ttl=24h A role says which names a caller may request and the longest lifetime. Policies decide who may use a role. A private CA with Vault Flow in order: 1, Root CA (offline) to Intermediate CA in Vault; 2, Intermediate CA in Vault to PKI role; 3, PKI role to Service; 4, Root CA (offline) to Trust bundle; 5, Service to Trust bundle. Security Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Root CA (offline) Security What it does: Signs the intermediate, then stays out of daily use. If it fails or is weak: A leaked root key means rebuilding all trust. Intermediate CA in Vault Security What it does: Signs service certificates. If it fails or is weak: Its key is the one used every day. PKI role Step What it does: Limits domains, key types and TTL. If it fails or is weak: A wildcard role can issue for any name. Service Service What it does: Requests and renews its certificate. If it fails or is weak: Missing renewal still causes expiry. Trust bundle Data store What it does: The root certificate distributed to clients. If it fails or is weak: Clients that lack it reject the chain. Issuing and renewing Applications ask for a certificate, or a sidecar or agent does it and writes files for the service. Because lifetimes are short, the agent renews at about two thirds of the lifetime. Cert-manager in Kubernetes can use Vault as an issuer. Revocation With a 24 hour life, waiting for expiry is often enough. For longer lifetimes Vault keeps a CRL and can serve OCSP. Newer Vault versions support ACME, so standard ACME clients can request certificates from your internal CA. Incident: The weekend expiry Incident An internal API stopped because its certificate had expired. $ echo | openssl s_client -connect api.internal:443 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate notAfter=Nov 2 00:00:00 2026 GMT $ date -u Sat Nov 7 08:10:00 UTC 2026 The certificate had a one year lifetime and renewal was a manual calendar task nobody owned. The team moved to Vault issued certificates with a 24 hour TTL, renewed by an agent every 16 hours. Common mistakes Issuing leaf certificates directly from the root Roles that allow any domain Long lifetimes without a revocation plan Not alerting when renewal fails"},"vault/transit-encryption-as-a-service":{"html":"\u003cp>Encryption is easy to call and hard to do well. Key storage, rotation and access control are where teams fail. Transit moves all of that out of the application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Encrypting and decrypting through the API\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key versions, rotation and rewrap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data keys for large payloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policies that separate encrypt from decrypt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"basic-use\">Basic use\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault secrets enable transit\nvault write -f transit/keys/orders\nPT=$(echo -n &quot;4111 1111 1111 1111&quot; | base64)\nvault write transit/encrypt/orders plaintext=&quot;$PT&quot;\nvault write transit/decrypt/orders ciphertext=&quot;vault:v1:abc123example&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault secrets \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> transit\nvault write -f transit/keys/orders\nPT=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;4111 1111 1111 1111&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span>)\nvault write transit/encrypt/orders plaintext=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PT\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\nvault write transit/decrypt/orders ciphertext=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vault:v1:abc123example&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Plaintext is base64 encoded in and out. The result looks like \u003ccode>vault:v1:...\u003c/code>, where \u003ccode>v1\u003c/code> is the key version that encrypted it. Store the ciphertext in your database. The key never leaves Vault.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation-and-rewrap\">Rotation and rewrap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault write -f transit/keys/orders/rotate\nvault write transit/rewrap/orders ciphertext=&quot;vault:v1:abc123example&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault write -f transit/keys/orders/rotate\nvault write transit/rewrap/orders ciphertext=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vault:v1:abc123example&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rotating creates a new key version for new encryptions; older versions still decrypt. \u003cstrong>Rewrap\u003c/strong> converts old ciphertext to the latest version inside Vault without exposing plaintext, so you can retire old key versions with \u003ccode>min_decryption_version\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"app&gt;tr;tr&gt;db;tr&gt;rot;pol&gt;tr\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Encrypting a field with transit\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tr\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vault transit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rot\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Key rotation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Separate policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to Vault transit; 2, Vault transit to Database; 3, Vault transit to Key rotation; 4, Separate policies to Vault transit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends plaintext, stores ciphertext.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logging the plaintext defeats the point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tr\">\u003cdt>Vault transit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the key and does the cryptography.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Availability matters, since reads need decrypt.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Contains only ciphertext.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A stolen dump alone is useless.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rot\">\u003cdt>Key rotation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> New versions for new data; rewrap for old data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping rewrap leaves old keys in use.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Separate policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Services that write need encrypt only.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One policy for both widens exposure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data-keys\">Data keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Sending large files to Vault is slow. Instead request a \u003cstrong>data key\u003c/strong> from \u003ccode>transit/datakey/plaintext/orders\u003c/code>: you get a plaintext key to encrypt locally and a wrapped copy to store beside the data. Discard the plaintext key after use. To decrypt, ask Vault to unwrap the stored copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policies\">Policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give writers only the \u003ccode>update\u003c/code> capability on \u003ccode>transit/encrypt/orders\u003c/code> and the few services that must read the data \u003ccode>transit/decrypt/orders\u003c/code>. A service that cannot decrypt cannot be tricked into leaking plaintext.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-dump-that-was-only-ciphertext\">Incident: The dump that was only ciphertext\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database backup was exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;select card from payments limit 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select card from payments limit 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">                      card\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> vault:v3:8SDd3WHDOjf7mq69CyCqY...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> vault:v3:Zk0mFQd3pT1xA2lU91nBc...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A misconfigured storage bucket exposed a database backup. Because card numbers were encrypted through transit, the backup contained only ciphertext, and the key was never in the dump. The incident was contained without notifying customers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging plaintext before encrypting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One policy for encrypt and decrypt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rotating keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending very large payloads through the API\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"basic-use","text":"Basic use"},{"id":"rotation-and-rewrap","text":"Rotation and rewrap"},{"id":"data-keys","text":"Data keys"},{"id":"policies","text":"Policies"},{"id":"incident-the-dump-that-was-only-ciphertext","text":"Incident: The dump that was only ciphertext"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Transit secrets engine","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/secrets/transit"},{"t":"Encryption as a service","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/tutorials/encryption-as-a-service/eaas-transit"},{"t":"Key rotation","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/api-docs/secret/transit"}],"text":"Encryption is easy to call and hard to do well. Key storage, rotation and access control are where teams fail. Transit moves all of that out of the application. What you will learn Encrypting and decrypting through the API Key versions, rotation and rewrap Data keys for large payloads Policies that separate encrypt from decrypt Basic use vault secrets enable transit vault write -f transit/keys/orders PT=$( echo -n \"4111 1111 1111 1111\" | base64 ) vault write transit/encrypt/orders plaintext= \" $PT \" vault write transit/decrypt/orders ciphertext= \"vault:v1:abc123example\" Plaintext is base64 encoded in and out. The result looks like vault:v1:... , where v1 is the key version that encrypted it. Store the ciphertext in your database. The key never leaves Vault. Rotation and rewrap vault write -f transit/keys/orders/rotate vault write transit/rewrap/orders ciphertext= \"vault:v1:abc123example\" Rotating creates a new key version for new encryptions; older versions still decrypt. Rewrap converts old ciphertext to the latest version inside Vault without exposing plaintext, so you can retire old key versions with min_decryption_version . Encrypting a field with transit Flow in order: 1, Application to Vault transit; 2, Vault transit to Database; 3, Vault transit to Key rotation; 4, Separate policies to Vault transit. Client Security Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Client What it does: Sends plaintext, stores ciphertext. If it fails or is weak: Logging the plaintext defeats the point. Vault transit Security What it does: Holds the key and does the cryptography. If it fails or is weak: Availability matters, since reads need decrypt. Database Data store What it does: Contains only ciphertext. If it fails or is weak: A stolen dump alone is useless. Key rotation Step What it does: New versions for new data; rewrap for old data. If it fails or is weak: Skipping rewrap leaves old keys in use. Separate policies Security What it does: Services that write need encrypt only. If it fails or is weak: One policy for both widens exposure. Data keys Sending large files to Vault is slow. Instead request a data key from transit/datakey/plaintext/orders : you get a plaintext key to encrypt locally and a wrapped copy to store beside the data. Discard the plaintext key after use. To decrypt, ask Vault to unwrap the stored copy. Policies Give writers only the update capability on transit/encrypt/orders and the few services that must read the data transit/decrypt/orders . A service that cannot decrypt cannot be tricked into leaking plaintext. Incident: The dump that was only ciphertext Incident A database backup was exposed. $ psql -c \"select card from payments limit 2\" card --------------------------------------------- vault:v3:8SDd3WHDOjf7mq69CyCqY... vault:v3:Zk0mFQd3pT1xA2lU91nBc... A misconfigured storage bucket exposed a database backup. Because card numbers were encrypted through transit, the backup contained only ciphertext, and the key was never in the dump. The incident was contained without notifying customers. Common mistakes Logging plaintext before encrypting One policy for encrypt and decrypt Never rotating keys Sending very large payloads through the API"},"vault/vault-on-kubernetes-injector-csi-and-operators":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes Secrets are only base64 encoded and are easy to over-share. Vault can supply secrets at runtime, with each pod proving its identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Kubernetes authentication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The Agent Injector sidecar\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CSI provider and Vault Secrets Operator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing between the patterns\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"login\">Login\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable the Kubernetes auth method and create a role that binds a service account and namespace to a policy. The pod presents its service account token, Vault asks the Kubernetes API to validate it, and returns a token with the role&#39;s policies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault auth enable kubernetes\nvault write auth/kubernetes/role/orders \\\n  bound_service_account_names=orders \\\n  bound_service_account_namespaces=shop \\\n  policies=orders-read ttl=1h\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault auth \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> kubernetes\nvault write auth/kubernetes/role/orders \\\n  bound_service_account_names=orders \\\n  bound_service_account_namespaces=shop \\\n  policies=orders-read ttl=1h\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"agent-injector\">Agent Injector\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Annotations on a pod tell a mutating webhook to add a Vault Agent sidecar and init container.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"metadata:\n  annotations:\n    vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject: &quot;true&quot;\n    vault.hashicorp.com/role: &quot;orders&quot;\n    vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject-secret-db: &quot;database/creds/orders-ro&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;true&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vault.hashicorp.com/role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject-secret-db:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database/creds/orders-ro&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The agent logs in, fetches the secret, writes it to \u003ccode>/vault/secrets/db\u003c/code>, and renews it while the pod runs. The application only reads a file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;inj;pod&gt;csi;pod&gt;vso;inj&gt;vault;csi&gt;vault;vso&gt;vault\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Three ways to deliver secrets to a pod\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod with service account\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inj\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Agent Injector sidecar\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"csi\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CSI provider\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vso\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vault Secrets Operator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vault\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Vault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod with service account to Agent Injector sidecar; 2, Pod with service account to CSI provider; 3, Pod with service account to Vault Secrets Operator; 4, Agent Injector sidecar to Vault; 5, CSI provider to Vault; 6, Vault Secrets Operator to Vault.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod with service account \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Proves identity to Vault.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A shared service account weakens bindings.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inj\">\u003cdt>Agent Injector sidecar \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes secrets to a shared volume and renews them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Adds a sidecar to every pod.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"csi\">\u003cdt>CSI provider \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Mounts secrets as a volume at pod start.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rotation needs the driver to remount.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vso\">\u003cdt>Vault Secrets Operator \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Syncs secrets into Kubernetes Secrets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The Secret lives in the cluster again.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vault\">\u003cdt>Vault \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates the identity and returns secrets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unavailable Vault stops new pods.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operator-and-external-secrets\">Operator and External Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Vault Secrets Operator\u003c/strong> watches resources such as \u003ccode>VaultStaticSecret\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>VaultDynamicSecret\u003c/code>, and writes the result into a Kubernetes Secret that pods consume the usual way. \u003cstrong>External Secrets Operator\u003c/strong> offers a similar model for many backends, including Vault and cloud managers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Need a file and rotation without code changes: injector or CSI. Need a native Kubernetes Secret for an existing chart: operator. Remember that any secret copied into a Kubernetes Secret is only as safe as your cluster access control and etcd encryption.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-pod-that-stayed-pending\">Incident: A pod that stayed Pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new pod never started because the secret volume failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod orders-7f9c | tail -n 3\nvault read auth/kubernetes/role/orders | grep bound_service_account_names\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod orders-7f9c | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning  FailedMount  2m  kubelet  MountVolume.SetUp failed: permission denied\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> auth/kubernetes/role/orders | grep bound_service_account_names\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bound_service_account_names    [order]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The role was bound to a service account named order, but the pod used orders. The login was denied and the init container never finished. Correcting the binding fixed the Pending pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Binding roles to every service account\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying all secrets into Kubernetes Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping etcd encryption when you use synced Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using one service account for many apps\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"login","text":"Login"},{"id":"agent-injector","text":"Agent Injector"},{"id":"operator-and-external-secrets","text":"Operator and External Secrets"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-a-pod-that-stayed-pending","text":"Incident: A pod that stayed Pending"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Vault on Kubernetes","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/platform/k8s"},{"t":"Vault Secrets Operator","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/platform/k8s/vso"},{"t":"Agent Injector","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/platform/k8s/injector"}],"text":"Kubernetes Secrets are only base64 encoded and are easy to over-share. Vault can supply secrets at runtime, with each pod proving its identity. What you will learn Kubernetes authentication The Agent Injector sidecar CSI provider and Vault Secrets Operator Choosing between the patterns Login Enable the Kubernetes auth method and create a role that binds a service account and namespace to a policy. The pod presents its service account token, Vault asks the Kubernetes API to validate it, and returns a token with the role&#39;s policies. vault auth enable kubernetes vault write auth/kubernetes/role/orders \\ bound_service_account_names=orders \\ bound_service_account_namespaces=shop \\ policies=orders-read ttl=1h Agent Injector Annotations on a pod tell a mutating webhook to add a Vault Agent sidecar and init container. metadata: annotations: vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject: \"true\" vault.hashicorp.com/role: \"orders\" vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject-secret-db: \"database/creds/orders-ro\" The agent logs in, fetches the secret, writes it to /vault/secrets/db , and renews it while the pod runs. The application only reads a file. Three ways to deliver secrets to a pod Flow in order: 1, Pod with service account to Agent Injector sidecar; 2, Pod with service account to CSI provider; 3, Pod with service account to Vault Secrets Operator; 4, Agent Injector sidecar to Vault; 5, CSI provider to Vault; 6, Vault Secrets Operator to Vault. Client Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod with service account Client What it does: Proves identity to Vault. If it fails or is weak: A shared service account weakens bindings. Agent Injector sidecar Step What it does: Writes secrets to a shared volume and renews them. If it fails or is weak: Adds a sidecar to every pod. CSI provider Step What it does: Mounts secrets as a volume at pod start. If it fails or is weak: Rotation needs the driver to remount. Vault Secrets Operator Step What it does: Syncs secrets into Kubernetes Secrets. If it fails or is weak: The Secret lives in the cluster again. Vault Security What it does: Validates the identity and returns secrets. If it fails or is weak: Unavailable Vault stops new pods. Operator and External Secrets The Vault Secrets Operator watches resources such as VaultStaticSecret and VaultDynamicSecret , and writes the result into a Kubernetes Secret that pods consume the usual way. External Secrets Operator offers a similar model for many backends, including Vault and cloud managers. Choosing Need a file and rotation without code changes: injector or CSI. Need a native Kubernetes Secret for an existing chart: operator. Remember that any secret copied into a Kubernetes Secret is only as safe as your cluster access control and etcd encryption. Incident: A pod that stayed Pending Incident A new pod never started because the secret volume failed. $ kubectl describe pod orders-7f9c | tail -n 3 Warning FailedMount 2m kubelet MountVolume.SetUp failed: permission denied $ vault read auth/kubernetes/role/orders | grep bound_service_account_names bound_service_account_names [order] The role was bound to a service account named order, but the pod used orders. The login was denied and the init container never finished. Correcting the binding fixed the Pending pod. Common mistakes Binding roles to every service account Copying all secrets into Kubernetes Secrets Skipping etcd encryption when you use synced Secrets Using one service account for many apps"},"vault/vault-high-availability-raft-and-disaster-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Because everything depends on Vault, its availability sets the availability of every application and pipeline that reads secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Active and standby nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a Raft cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Snapshots and restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication and disaster recovery options\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"active-and-standby\">Active and standby\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In a cluster one node is \u003cstrong>active\u003c/strong> and handles all writes. Standby nodes forward requests to it or redirect clients. If the active node fails, the standbys elect a new leader within seconds. Clients should use a load balancer or an address that follows the active node, with health checks on \u003ccode>/v1/sys/health\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"raft\">Raft\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault operator raft list-peers\nvault operator raft join https://vault-1.internal:8200\nvault operator raft snapshot save backup.snap\nvault operator raft snapshot restore backup.snap\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault operator raft list-peers\nvault operator raft \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">join\u003c/span> https://vault-1.internal:8200\nvault operator raft snapshot save backup.snap\nvault operator raft snapshot restore backup.snap\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Raft needs a majority to commit: three nodes survive one failure, five survive two. Spread nodes over three zones. Use fast local disks and a low latency network, since each write is replicated before it is acknowledged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"lb&gt;act;act&gt;s1;act&gt;s2;act&gt;snap\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A three node Vault cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Active node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"s1\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Standby 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"s2\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Standby 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"snap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Snapshot storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Active node; 2, Active node to Standby 1; 3, Active node to Standby 2; 4, Active node to Snapshot storage.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends clients to a healthy node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Needs a health check on the active node.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Active node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Handles writes and replicates them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Losing it triggers an election.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"s1\">\u003cdt>Standby 1 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replicates data and can become active.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Needs the same unseal method.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"s2\">\u003cdt>Standby 2 \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replicates data and can become active.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zone loss with a bad layout loses quorum.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"snap\">\u003cdt>Snapshot storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypted snapshots in another location.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Untested snapshots may not restore.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots-and-restore\">Snapshots and restore\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automatic snapshots (Enterprise) or a scheduled job produce files you copy to another place. Snapshots contain encrypted data, so you also need the unseal key or KMS access in the recovery site. Practise a restore into an empty cluster regularly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication\">Replication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vault Enterprise offers \u003cstrong>performance replication\u003c/strong> (secondary clusters serve reads close to users) and \u003cstrong>disaster recovery replication\u003c/strong> (a warm standby cluster promoted if the primary fails). In the community edition, rely on snapshots and a documented rebuild procedure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"maintenance\">Maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Upgrade standbys first, then step down the active node to move leadership, then upgrade it. Watch \u003ccode>vault.raft.leader.lastContact\u003c/code> and apply latency.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-lost-quorum-in-a-bad-layout\">Incident: Lost quorum in a bad layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both standby nodes were in the same zone as the failed active node.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault operator raft list-peers\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault operator raft list-peers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Node     Address               State     Voter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vault-1  10.0.1.11:8201        follower  true\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vault-2  10.0.1.12:8201        (unreachable)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vault-3  10.0.1.13:8201        (unreachable)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All three nodes had been placed in one availability zone to save cross zone traffic cost. When the zone failed, the cluster lost quorum. The team spread the nodes over three zones and tested a zone failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting all nodes in one failure domain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking snapshots but never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using two nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading the active node first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"active-and-standby","text":"Active and standby"},{"id":"raft","text":"Raft"},{"id":"snapshots-and-restore","text":"Snapshots and restore"},{"id":"replication","text":"Replication"},{"id":"maintenance","text":"Maintenance"},{"id":"incident-lost-quorum-in-a-bad-layout","text":"Incident: Lost quorum in a bad layout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Vault high availability","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/ha"},{"t":"Raft snapshots","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/commands/operator/raft"},{"t":"Production hardening","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/production-hardening"}],"text":"Because everything depends on Vault, its availability sets the availability of every application and pipeline that reads secrets. What you will learn Active and standby nodes Running a Raft cluster Snapshots and restore Replication and disaster recovery options Active and standby In a cluster one node is active and handles all writes. Standby nodes forward requests to it or redirect clients. If the active node fails, the standbys elect a new leader within seconds. Clients should use a load balancer or an address that follows the active node, with health checks on /v1/sys/health . Raft vault operator raft list-peers vault operator raft join https://vault-1.internal:8200 vault operator raft snapshot save backup.snap vault operator raft snapshot restore backup.snap Raft needs a majority to commit: three nodes survive one failure, five survive two. Spread nodes over three zones. Use fast local disks and a low latency network, since each write is replicated before it is acknowledged. A three node Vault cluster Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Active node; 2, Active node to Standby 1; 3, Active node to Standby 2; 4, Active node to Snapshot storage. Network edge Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Load balancer Network edge What it does: Sends clients to a healthy node. If it fails or is weak: Needs a health check on the active node. Active node Security What it does: Handles writes and replicates them. If it fails or is weak: Losing it triggers an election. Standby 1 Security What it does: Replicates data and can become active. If it fails or is weak: Needs the same unseal method. Standby 2 Security What it does: Replicates data and can become active. If it fails or is weak: Zone loss with a bad layout loses quorum. Snapshot storage Data store What it does: Encrypted snapshots in another location. If it fails or is weak: Untested snapshots may not restore. Snapshots and restore Automatic snapshots (Enterprise) or a scheduled job produce files you copy to another place. Snapshots contain encrypted data, so you also need the unseal key or KMS access in the recovery site. Practise a restore into an empty cluster regularly. Replication Vault Enterprise offers performance replication (secondary clusters serve reads close to users) and disaster recovery replication (a warm standby cluster promoted if the primary fails). In the community edition, rely on snapshots and a documented rebuild procedure. Maintenance Upgrade standbys first, then step down the active node to move leadership, then upgrade it. Watch vault.raft.leader.lastContact and apply latency. Incident: Lost quorum in a bad layout Incident Both standby nodes were in the same zone as the failed active node. $ vault operator raft list-peers Node Address State Voter vault-1 10.0.1.11:8201 follower true vault-2 10.0.1.12:8201 (unreachable) vault-3 10.0.1.13:8201 (unreachable) All three nodes had been placed in one availability zone to save cross zone traffic cost. When the zone failed, the cluster lost quorum. The team spread the nodes over three zones and tested a zone failure. Common mistakes Putting all nodes in one failure domain Taking snapshots but never testing a restore Using two nodes Upgrading the active node first"},"vault/audit-logging-monitoring-and-hardening-vault":{"html":"\u003cp>A secrets manager is a high value target. The same properties that make it useful, central access to many secrets, make careful logging, monitoring and hardening essential.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Audit devices and what they record\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metrics and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Production hardening checklist\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Operating without root tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"audit\">Audit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault audit enable file file_path=/var/log/vault/audit.log\nvault audit enable syslog tag=vault\nvault audit list\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault audit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> file file_path=/var/log/vault/audit.log\nvault audit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">enable\u003c/span> syslog tag=vault\nvault audit list\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each request and response is logged as JSON with sensitive values HMAC hashed. You can search for who read a path and when, without storing the secrets in the log. If every audit device fails to write, Vault blocks requests rather than operate without a record, so enable two and monitor disk space. Ship logs to central storage that Vault admins cannot edit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monitoring\">Monitoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable Prometheus telemetry and alert on: Vault sealed, no leader or frequent leader changes, high request error rates, rising token or lease counts, slow storage (Raft apply time), and certificate expiry of the Vault listener.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"seal&gt;lead;lead&gt;lease;aud&gt;lease;lease&gt;sec\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What to watch and why\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"seal\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sealed state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lead\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Leader and quorum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit device health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lease\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Leases and tokens\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hardening\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Sealed state to Leader and quorum; 2, Leader and quorum to Leases and tokens; 3, Audit device health to Leases and tokens; 4, Leases and tokens to Hardening.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"seal\">\u003cdt>Sealed state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Vault cannot serve when sealed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A silent seal looks like a network outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lead\">\u003cdt>Leader and quorum \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A stable leader means writes work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Flapping leaders point to network or disk issues.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit device health \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> If it fails, requests are blocked.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A full disk is the usual cause.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lease\">\u003cdt>Leases and tokens \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Counts show leaks and runaway clients.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Millions of leases slow Vault.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Hardening \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limits what an attacker gets from a node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped controls mean a bigger breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hardening-checklist\">Hardening checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>TLS everywhere, with a certificate from your CA.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No root token in daily use; revoke after setup, and generate one only for emergencies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run Vault as a dedicated non-root user, with core dumps and swap disabled, and \u003ccode>mlock\u003c/code> enabled.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Firewall the API and cluster ports to known clients.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Auto-unseal with a KMS key that has tight access.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate policies for operators and applications, with \u003ccode>sudo\u003c/code> rare.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enable namespaces (Enterprise) or separate mounts per team.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep Vault updated.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"without-root-tokens\">Without root tokens\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you need privileged work, create a short-lived token with the required policy, or use \u003ccode>vault operator generate-root\u003c/code> with a quorum of key holders for emergencies. Record who did it and why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-audit-disk-filled\">Incident: The audit disk filled\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Vault stopped answering after a long weekend.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault read sys/health\ndf -h /var/log/vault\njournalctl -u vault | tail -n 1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> sys/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error reading sys/health: 500 internal server error\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/log/vault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda3  20G  20G  0  100% /var/log/vault\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u vault | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">audit: no audit devices are available to write to\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The audit log grew until the disk was full. With no working audit device Vault refused all requests. The team added a second audit device, log rotation and shipping, and a disk space alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Only one audit device\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving audit logs on the same disk as storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using root tokens for daily work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerting only on process up, not on sealed state\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"audit","text":"Audit"},{"id":"monitoring","text":"Monitoring"},{"id":"hardening-checklist","text":"Hardening checklist"},{"id":"without-root-tokens","text":"Without root tokens"},{"id":"incident-the-audit-disk-filled","text":"Incident: The audit disk filled"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Audit devices","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/audit"},{"t":"Telemetry","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/internals/telemetry"},{"t":"Production hardening","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/production-hardening"}],"text":"A secrets manager is a high value target. The same properties that make it useful, central access to many secrets, make careful logging, monitoring and hardening essential. What you will learn Audit devices and what they record Metrics and alerts Production hardening checklist Operating without root tokens Audit vault audit enable file file_path=/var/log/vault/audit.log vault audit enable syslog tag=vault vault audit list Each request and response is logged as JSON with sensitive values HMAC hashed. You can search for who read a path and when, without storing the secrets in the log. If every audit device fails to write, Vault blocks requests rather than operate without a record, so enable two and monitor disk space. Ship logs to central storage that Vault admins cannot edit. Monitoring Enable Prometheus telemetry and alert on: Vault sealed, no leader or frequent leader changes, high request error rates, rising token or lease counts, slow storage (Raft apply time), and certificate expiry of the Vault listener. What to watch and why Flow in order: 1, Sealed state to Leader and quorum; 2, Leader and quorum to Leases and tokens; 3, Audit device health to Leases and tokens; 4, Leases and tokens to Hardening. Observability Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Sealed state Observability What it does: Vault cannot serve when sealed. If it fails or is weak: A silent seal looks like a network outage. Leader and quorum Observability What it does: A stable leader means writes work. If it fails or is weak: Flapping leaders point to network or disk issues. Audit device health Observability What it does: If it fails, requests are blocked. If it fails or is weak: A full disk is the usual cause. Leases and tokens Observability What it does: Counts show leaks and runaway clients. If it fails or is weak: Millions of leases slow Vault. Hardening Security What it does: Limits what an attacker gets from a node. If it fails or is weak: Skipped controls mean a bigger breach. Hardening checklist TLS everywhere, with a certificate from your CA. No root token in daily use; revoke after setup, and generate one only for emergencies. Run Vault as a dedicated non-root user, with core dumps and swap disabled, and mlock enabled. Firewall the API and cluster ports to known clients. Auto-unseal with a KMS key that has tight access. Separate policies for operators and applications, with sudo rare. Enable namespaces (Enterprise) or separate mounts per team. Keep Vault updated. Without root tokens If you need privileged work, create a short-lived token with the required policy, or use vault operator generate-root with a quorum of key holders for emergencies. Record who did it and why. Incident: The audit disk filled Incident Vault stopped answering after a long weekend. $ vault read sys/health Error reading sys/health: 500 internal server error $ df -h /var/log/vault /dev/sda3 20G 20G 0 100% /var/log/vault $ journalctl -u vault | tail -n 1 audit: no audit devices are available to write to The audit log grew until the disk was full. With no working audit device Vault refused all requests. The team added a second audit device, log rotation and shipping, and a disk space alert. Common mistakes Only one audit device Leaving audit logs on the same disk as storage Using root tokens for daily work Alerting only on process up, not on sealed state"},"vault/incident-response-rotating-and-revoking-secrets":{"html":"\u003cp>Incidents with secrets are time critical. A short, practised sequence is better than clever improvisation: identify, contain, rotate, investigate and learn.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Identify and revoke a leaked token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Revoke leases by prefix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotate static secrets and downstream systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Emergency options and the review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contain\">Contain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You may not know who owns a leaked token. The \u003cstrong>accessor\u003c/strong> is a safe reference to it: audit logs show accessors, not tokens.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault token lookup -accessor &quot;$ACCESSOR&quot;\nvault token revoke -accessor &quot;$ACCESSOR&quot;\nvault lease revoke -prefix database/creds/orders-ro\nvault lease revoke -prefix aws/creds/deploy\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">vault token lookup -accessor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ACCESSOR\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\nvault token revoke -accessor \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ACCESSOR\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\nvault lease revoke -prefix database/creds/orders-ro\nvault lease revoke -prefix aws/creds/deploy\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Revoking a token also revokes child tokens and the leases they hold, which removes dynamic credentials issued to it. Revoking by prefix is a broad sweep when you do not know which lease leaked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotate\">Rotate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Static secrets in KV that the token could read must be assumed exposed. Replace them at the source (the vendor dashboard, the database), update KV, and restart consumers. Rotate the database root credential if the database engine was reachable. Use \u003ccode>vault write -f transit/keys/&lt;name&gt;/rotate\u003c/code> for transit keys, then rewrap.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"det&gt;rev;rev&gt;aud;aud&gt;rot;aud&gt;rev2\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Response to a leaked token\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"det\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Detect\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Revoke token and leases\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read the audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rot\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rotate exposed secrets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review and fix the cause\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Detect to Revoke token and leases; 2, Revoke token and leases to Read the audit log; 3, Read the audit log to Rotate exposed secrets; 4, Read the audit log to Review and fix the cause.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"det\">\u003cdt>Detect \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A scanner, alert or report shows a leaked token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow detection means a wider blast radius.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Revoke token and leases \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stops further use and drops dynamic credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Revoking the wrong token breaks applications.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Read the audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows which paths the token touched.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Logs show accessors, not token values.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rot\">\u003cdt>Rotate exposed secrets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replace static secrets at the source.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing a downstream copy leaves the leak open.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev2\">\u003cdt>Review and fix the cause \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shorten TTLs, narrow policies, add scanning.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it the next leak repeats.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"investigate\">Investigate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Search the audit log for the token accessor and the client IP. List the paths it read. For each static secret that was read, treat it as exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"emergency-options\">Emergency options\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>vault operator seal\u003c/code> makes Vault stop serving all requests at once; it is a last resort because every application loses access, and unsealing needs your key holders or KMS. Rotating the root key (\u003ccode>vault operator rotate\u003c/code>) and re-keying (\u003ccode>vault operator rekey\u003c/code>) change encryption and unseal keys after a suspected compromise of key shares.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"after\">After\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a short review: how the secret leaked, how long it was valid, what it could reach, and what stops it next time. Common fixes: shorter TTLs, narrower policies, secret scanning in repositories, and dynamic secrets instead of static.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-token-in-the-public-repository\">Incident: The token in the public repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A token was found in a public commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"vault token lookup -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example\nvault token revoke -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault token lookup -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">policies     [ci-read]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ttl          718h\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>vault token revoke -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Success! Revoked token (if it existed)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The token had a thirty day TTL and the policy ci-read. Revoking it and its leases took two minutes. The audit log showed one read of a test path, so the team rotated one static secret and added secret scanning to the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Revoking blindly without knowing what depends on the token\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotating Vault but not the downstream systems\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sealing Vault first and asking questions later\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"contain","text":"Contain"},{"id":"rotate","text":"Rotate"},{"id":"investigate","text":"Investigate"},{"id":"emergency-options","text":"Emergency options"},{"id":"after","text":"After"},{"id":"incident-the-token-in-the-public-repository","text":"Incident: The token in the public repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Revoke tokens and leases","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/lease"},{"t":"Token accessors","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/concepts/tokens"},{"t":"Rekey and rotate","u":"https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/commands/operator/rekey"}],"text":"Incidents with secrets are time critical. A short, practised sequence is better than clever improvisation: identify, contain, rotate, investigate and learn. What you will learn Identify and revoke a leaked token Revoke leases by prefix Rotate static secrets and downstream systems Emergency options and the review Contain You may not know who owns a leaked token. The accessor is a safe reference to it: audit logs show accessors, not tokens. vault token lookup -accessor \" $ACCESSOR \" vault token revoke -accessor \" $ACCESSOR \" vault lease revoke -prefix database/creds/orders-ro vault lease revoke -prefix aws/creds/deploy Revoking a token also revokes child tokens and the leases they hold, which removes dynamic credentials issued to it. Revoking by prefix is a broad sweep when you do not know which lease leaked. Rotate Static secrets in KV that the token could read must be assumed exposed. Replace them at the source (the vendor dashboard, the database), update KV, and restart consumers. Rotate the database root credential if the database engine was reachable. Use vault write -f transit/keys/\u003cname>/rotate for transit keys, then rewrap. Response to a leaked token Flow in order: 1, Detect to Revoke token and leases; 2, Revoke token and leases to Read the audit log; 3, Read the audit log to Rotate exposed secrets; 4, Read the audit log to Review and fix the cause. Observability Step Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Detect Observability What it does: A scanner, alert or report shows a leaked token. If it fails or is weak: Slow detection means a wider blast radius. Revoke token and leases Step What it does: Stops further use and drops dynamic credentials. If it fails or is weak: Revoking the wrong token breaks applications. Read the audit log Observability What it does: Shows which paths the token touched. If it fails or is weak: Logs show accessors, not token values. Rotate exposed secrets Security What it does: Replace static secrets at the source. If it fails or is weak: Missing a downstream copy leaves the leak open. Review and fix the cause Step What it does: Shorten TTLs, narrow policies, add scanning. If it fails or is weak: Without it the next leak repeats. Investigate Search the audit log for the token accessor and the client IP. List the paths it read. For each static secret that was read, treat it as exposed. Emergency options vault operator seal makes Vault stop serving all requests at once; it is a last resort because every application loses access, and unsealing needs your key holders or KMS. Rotating the root key ( vault operator rotate ) and re-keying ( vault operator rekey ) change encryption and unseal keys after a suspected compromise of key shares. After Write a short review: how the secret leaked, how long it was valid, what it could reach, and what stops it next time. Common fixes: shorter TTLs, narrower policies, secret scanning in repositories, and dynamic secrets instead of static. Incident: The token in the public repository Incident A token was found in a public commit. $ vault token lookup -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example policies [ci-read] ttl 718h $ vault token revoke -accessor 7c1a3d6e-example Success! Revoked token (if it existed) The token had a thirty day TTL and the policy ci-read. Revoking it and its leases took two minutes. The audit log showed one read of a test path, so the team rotated one static secret and added secret scanning to the repository. Common mistakes Revoking blindly without knowing what depends on the token Rotating Vault but not the downstream systems Sealing Vault first and asking questions later Skipping the review"}});